Abstract:
Abstract

&lt;div&gt;
 Bankruptcy prediction models typically rely on financial ratios derived from accounting statements that are prone to error and manipulation.&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;We consider two novel sets of variables for bankruptcy prediction: conformity of values in statements with Benford's law and incidence of bankruptcy-unrelated litigation. The former relies on the long known observation that deviations from Benford's law may signal data manipulation. The latter builds on the intuition that would-be bankrupts face litigation related to their contractual non-performance before filing for bankruptcy. In an original data set covering the near-universe of 2 mln Russian firms in 2012–2018 we find feature importance and predictive capability of litigation variables on par to most of the traditionally used financial ratios, both for larger firms and small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs). Conformity of statements with Benford's law is revealed as a modest bankruptcy predictor. We estimate that lenders of larger firms employing gradient-boosted decision tree models based on traditional financial ratios could have increased their Expected Maximum Profit by USD845 mln in 2018 in Russia have they added litigation features in their scoring. SMEs lenders gain is estimated at only USD14 mln. This suggests that the search for improved SMEs' bankruptcy models may have little to no practical significance in certain real-life contexts.&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
